# Diagram cuts. Braid is not token-concatenative — everything-exact
# means an arbitrary text substring is not a program. What replaces it
# is better: reflected code is in SPINE NORMAL FORM (Code = a list of
# stages = the diagram's horizontal slices), and at that granularity
# the concatenative property is a theorem you can run:
#
#   HORIZONTAL: every stage-boundary cut of a well-typed program
#   yields two runnable pieces, and run(prefix) ; run(suffix) = run(whole).
#   VERTICAL:   atom slices WITHIN a stage are sub-tensors — runnable.
#   BOXING:     a Code slice becomes an Fn value without running
#               (deferred through a closure; the dynamic check rides
#               the railway at apply time).

def getCode = reflect ; ((c -> c) | drop ; nil) ; merge
def c = [1 2 >> + >> dup >> *] ; getCode          # 4 stages: [1 2] [+] [dup] [*]

# ---- horizontal cut soundness, at EVERY boundary k = 0..4 ------------
# run the prefix, feed its output to the suffix: always 9 = run(whole).
# The CUT TYPE is the interesting part.  Splitting a program names an
# intermediate the whole program never mentions, and it differs per cut:
# after 1 stage the wires are Int Int, after 2 just Int.  So each cut
# states its own pair of witnesses — the prefix's expectation and the
# suffix's — and both are checked when the halves run.  That obligation
# is the honest price of cutting: the type between the halves is a fact
# about the cut, not about the program, so someone has to say it.
def cutAt =
    pre suf k ->
    (pre) (k c ; take) ; evalAs
    ((suf) (k c ; skip) ... ; evalAs ; (print | forget) ; merge | forget) ; merge

[pass]                [1 2 ; + ; dup ; *] 0 ; cutAt   # 9  (nothing | everything)
[1 2]                 [+ ; dup ; *]       1 ; cutAt   # 9  (cut mid-arithmetic)
[1 2 ; +]             [dup ; *]           2 ; cutAt   # 9
[1 2 ; + ; dup]       [*]                 3 ; cutAt   # 9
[1 2 ; + ; dup ; *]   [pass]                4 ; cutAt   # 9  (everything | nothing)

# ---- vertical cuts: slice atoms inside one stage ---------------------
def stage0 = c ; uncons ; (nil | (s r -> s)) ; merge      # the stage [1, 2]
[1] (1 stage0 ; take ; single) ; evalAs ; (print | forget) ; merge    # 1
[2] (1 stage0 ; skip ; single) ; evalAs ; (print | forget) ; merge    # 2

# a consuming stage cuts too: [dup drop] : a b ⇒ a a — take just [dup],
# now a program of its own (: a ⇒ a a), cut free of its stage-mate
def s2 = ([dup drop] ; getCode) ; uncons ; (nil | (s r -> s)) ; merge
7 ; [dup] (1 s2 ; take ; single) ... ; evalAs
    (_ print ; print | forget) ; merge                    # 7 7

# ---- boxing: cut a slice, box it, use it LATER as a value ------------
# Deferring the RUN no longer costs the result's TYPE.  `box` takes the
# WITNESS beside the code, so the boxed program is ordinarily typed and
# its result is ordinarily usable — `print`, not just `forget`.  The
# witness is never applied; it is the expectation the code must meet,
# spelled as a value because there is no type syntax inside terms.  It
# is also the fallback: on a miss it is still sitting there to run.
def box = (w cd -> [(w) (cd) ... ; evalAs])
[0] (2 c ; take) ; box              # Fn⟨ρ ⇒ (ρ Int | Str ρ)⟩ — unopened
apply ; (print | forget) ; merge    # 3
